# pssh — real-world examples

Parallel SSH tool family: `pssh` (run commands on many hosts), `pscp` (copy to
many hosts), `prsync` (rsync to many hosts), `pslurp` (copy FROM many hosts),
`pnuke` (kill processes on many hosts).

## Install

```sh
# Via pip (upstream instructions)
sudo pip install pssh

# On OpenSUSE Tumbleweed
sudo zypper install pssh
```

## Run a command on every host in a file

```sh
# -h hosts file, -l login user, -A ask for password, -i inline output per host
pssh -h pssh-hosts -l root -A -i "uptime"
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# hosts file: one host per line
printf 'homelab\nmediaserver\n' > ~/pssh-hosts

# Check pending updates on all Tumbleweed boxes
pssh -h ~/pssh-hosts -l david -i "zypper lu | tail -5"

# Restart a service everywhere, saving output per host
pssh -h ~/pssh-hosts -l david -o /tmp/pssh-out "sudo systemctl restart node_exporter"

# Push an updated config to all hosts
pscp -h ~/pssh-hosts -l david ~/configs/chrony.conf /tmp/chrony.conf

# Pull logs from every host into ./logs/<host>/
pslurp -h ~/pssh-hosts -l david -L ./logs /var/log/zypper.log zypper.log
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-h <file>` | Hosts file (one per line) |
| `-l <user>` | Default login user |
| `-i` | Inline: show stdout/stderr as each host completes |
| `-A` | Prompt for password and pass to ssh |
| `-t <sec>` | Per-host timeout |
| `-o <dir>` | Save stdout per host into directory |
| `-p <n>` | Max parallel connections |
